WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.823 --> 00:00:04.504
Kanban comes from Taiichi Ohno's Toyota Production System:

2
00:00:04.678 --> 00:00:06.268
a card with a bin of parts.

3
00:00:07.261 --> 00:00:07.946
Bin empty?

4
00:00:08.658 --> 00:00:10.434
The card goes back: make more.

5
00:00:11.162 --> 00:00:14.111
Consumption pulls production: no overproduction.

6
00:00:15.152 --> 00:00:19.204
In knowledge work Kanban makes the flow of work visible and steerable;

7
00:00:19.366 --> 00:00:21.061
David Anderson made it known.

8
00:00:22.062 --> 00:00:26.404
The Kanban Guide calls Kanban a strategy to optimise the flow of value,

9
00:00:26.497 --> 00:00:27.855
visual and pull-based.

10
00:00:29.209 --> 00:00:34.155
The Kanban board has a column per step: to do, analysis, build, test, done.

11
00:00:35.194 --> 00:00:39.525
Each work item, a request or a fault, is a card moving right.

12
00:00:40.612 --> 00:00:43.863
Start where you are: draw the work as it really flows.

13
00:00:44.586 --> 00:00:46.536
No fixed roles or sprints.

14
00:00:47.519 --> 00:00:52.163
The Definition of Workflow is the shared agreement: what an item is, start and done,

15
00:00:52.302 --> 00:00:54.996
steps, limits, rules, duration.

16
00:00:56.025 --> 00:00:58.986
A card that can't move on is blocked: a red sticker.

17
00:00:59.716 --> 00:01:02.189
These cards show where the system stutters.

18
00:01:03.188 --> 00:01:07.914
For you as a Lean consultant: visual management, and a blocked card is an andon.

19
00:01:09.229 --> 00:01:14.210
An honest difference: at Toyota a standard part, here every item differs.

20
00:01:14.924 --> 00:01:16.561
The card describes work.

21
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.841
Typical mistake: cards with no limit.

22
00:01:20.619 --> 00:01:23.475
Then it is a to-do list, not yet a pull system.

23
00:01:24.533 --> 00:01:29.699
Example: a machine builder's engineering department has forty loose sticky notes.

24
00:01:30.696 --> 00:01:36.071
Columns as the work really flows: requested, design, check, waiting for customer,

25
00:01:36.303 --> 00:01:36.826
released.

26
00:01:37.898 --> 00:01:43.041
An hour later all forty are up: twelve in design, nine waiting for the customer,

27
00:01:43.157 --> 00:01:44.887
six for over three weeks.

28
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:46.875
Four are blocked.

29
00:01:47.666 --> 00:01:51.985
Started: an engineer takes the card; done: the drawing is released.

30
00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:58.103
WIP is work in progress: started, not done, as in the factory.

31
00:01:58.768 --> 00:02:01.949
It costs money, hides problems, slows things.

32
00:02:02.976 --> 00:02:08.711
A WIP limit: a maximum per column or for the whole system, such as test at most two.

33
00:02:09.397 --> 00:02:09.722
Full?

34
00:02:10.477 --> 00:02:12.091
Nothing new: that is pull.

35
00:02:13.117 --> 00:02:17.598
Five things at once means switching, and context switching costs time.

36
00:02:18.316 --> 00:02:22.704
Gerald Weinberg estimated about twenty percent per extra project.

37
00:02:23.748 --> 00:02:26.186
Worse: each item sits idle longer.

38
00:02:26.917 --> 00:02:29.390
Everyone is busy; the customer waits.

39
00:02:30.709 --> 00:02:31.475
Column full?

40
00:02:32.161 --> 00:02:36.515
Pick nothing new; help finish further right: swarming, one item together.

41
00:02:37.557 --> 00:02:41.168
A developer helps test; an engineer does a colleague's check.

42
00:02:41.912 --> 00:02:43.572
Odd, but the team delivers more.

43
00:02:44.594 --> 00:02:49.447
The starting number: about the people in the step, or a few fewer, then adjust.

44
00:02:50.491 --> 00:02:53.045
For you: a supermarket or a fixed buffer.

45
00:02:53.819 --> 00:02:55.770
Work piles up at the bottleneck.

46
00:02:56.830 --> 00:03:01.219
Mistakes: stretching limits when busy, limits per person, not per step,

47
00:03:01.486 --> 00:03:03.181
and no rule for a full column.

48
00:03:04.203 --> 00:03:06.339
Some use one limit for the whole system.

49
00:03:07.068 --> 00:03:09.587
Ask: what do you do when the next column is full?

50
00:03:10.887 --> 00:03:16.611
Example: an IT team of five at a dairy plant, fifteen work items in progress.

51
00:03:17.321 --> 00:03:19.063
A request takes six weeks.

52
00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:23.443
Limits: build at most four, test at most two.

53
00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.117
Week one: test is full.

54
00:03:26.864 --> 00:03:27.990
A fifth in build?

55
00:03:28.766 --> 00:03:32.458
No: the developer helps test; an item is done that day.

56
00:03:33.446 --> 00:03:35.304
After six weeks: six at once,

57
00:03:35.409 --> 00:03:39.380
not fifteen, and a request takes about two and a half weeks.

58
00:03:40.678 --> 00:03:43.197
The Kanban Guide names four flow measures.

59
00:03:43.924 --> 00:03:48.022
First WIP and throughput: how many items finish per week.

60
00:03:49.054 --> 00:03:53.187
Work item age: how long an unfinished item has been under way.

61
00:03:54.168 --> 00:03:57.628
And cycle time: start to finish of a completed item,

62
00:03:57.837 --> 00:04:00.124
as your Definition of Workflow sets them.

63
00:04:01.176 --> 00:04:05.309
A trap: in Lean, cycle time is the time per unit at a station,

64
00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:07.085
say forty-five seconds.

65
00:04:07.798 --> 00:04:10.793
Kanban means what a factory calls throughput time.

66
00:04:11.783 --> 00:04:16.404
Many teams mean by lead time the time from the request, waiting included.

67
00:04:17.154 --> 00:04:18.292
Agree which you mean.

68
00:04:19.653 --> 00:04:21.058
Little's law, John Little,

69
00:04:21.221 --> 00:04:27.932
1961: average cycle time is average WIP divided by average throughput.

70
00:04:29.063 --> 00:04:33.567
Twelve items in progress, three a week done: four weeks on average.

71
00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:37.113
It holds for averages, not for one item.

72
00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:43.118
Halve the WIP at equal throughput, and the average cycle time halves.

73
00:04:43.835 --> 00:04:46.180
That is why WIP limits work.

74
00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:48.369
Condition: stable.

75
00:04:49.087 --> 00:04:53.383
What comes in goes out, on average; WIP stays about level.

76
00:04:54.071 --> 00:04:55.604
A growing pile breaks it.

77
00:04:56.717 --> 00:05:00.688
Vacanti stresses: a relation between averages after the fact,

78
00:05:00.874 --> 00:05:02.488
not a forecasting machine.

79
00:05:03.499 --> 00:05:05.717
Lean and Kanban are most alike here.

80
00:05:06.454 --> 00:05:08.161
Mistake: only the average.

81
00:05:08.891 --> 00:05:11.770
Look at the spread: where does the clock start and stop?

82
00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:17.427
Example: a furniture maker measures eight weeks: twelve in progress,

83
00:05:17.625 --> 00:05:20.086
three a week out, three in: stable.

84
00:05:21.074 --> 00:05:23.617
Twelve divided by three: four weeks.

85
00:05:24.382 --> 00:05:26.402
Measured four point two, a match.

86
00:05:27.118 --> 00:05:29.161
But too long for small requests.

87
00:05:30.162 --> 00:05:35.167
The WIP limit goes to six, still three a week done: two weeks, half.

88
00:05:36.147 --> 00:05:40.779
But watch: throughput stays at three a week, and no work runs past the board.

89
00:05:41.784 --> 00:05:43.177
More than three a week in?

90
00:05:43.862 --> 00:05:48.355
The queue before the board grows: waiting in lead time, not cycle time.
